GC and TRIM reclaiming real objects, on a real Guest.

host:     AWS i4i.metal, real KVM, Cloud Hypervisor
cache:    /var/lib/cats on the instance NVMe (/dev/nvme8n1, ext4)
store:    file adapter on that same NVMe (asserted before measuring)
path:     vhost-user (the path that advertises VIRTIO_BLK_F_DISCARD; guest
          discard_max_bytes = 33554432)
date_utc: 2026-07-27
volume:   vol_c5020850b919

The Guest rewrites the same 128 MiB file three times with O_DIRECT + sync. Each
rewrite publishes new objects and makes the previous ones unreachable, which is
the garbage a long-running Cat accumulates.

  start                  objects=36   size=61 MiB
  after rewrite 1        objects=74   size=191 MiB
  after rewrite 2        objects=113  size=325 MiB
  after rewrite 3        objects=149  size=454 MiB
  rm + fstrim            objects=152  size=454 MiB   <- fstrim reported 3.5 GiB trimmed
  before GC              objects=188  size=575 MiB
  after GC               objects=56   size=186 MiB

  GC report: data_live=56 data_deleted=133 nodes_live=5 nodes_deleted=25

Reading:

* 575 MiB -> 186 MiB on one volume: 389 MiB reclaimed, 3.1x. Before this branch
  nothing deleted any of it -- the objects stayed for the life of the volume.
* The object count does NOT fall at the fstrim line, and that is correct. TRIM
  turns the chunk's map entry into a Hole, which makes its objects unreachable;
  deleting them is GC's job. The +3 objects there are the commit that recorded
  the discard.
* `images/` still holds all 104 files afterwards. The shared golden root is
  referenced by this volume and has to survive its GC -- the property
  gc_never_deletes_a_shared_image_even_though_the_volume_references_it asserts in
  the unit tests, confirmed here against a real image on a real Guest.
* The 56 live objects are the volume's current content; the 133 deleted were
  superseded by later rewrites.

Method note: the first two attempts produced a flat line of identical numbers,
because the node was still configured for S3 while the script counted files in the
local blob directory, and because it guessed the volume id from a directory
listing containing stale volumes. Both are asserted before measuring now.
